WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:02.335
-You've been on the show
many times in the studio.

00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:03.269 line:92%
You are our first remote guest

00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:04.671 align:start size:94% position:6%
Thank you so much
for making the time.

00:00:04.671 --> 00:00:05.939 align:start size:94% position:6%
You are in Vermont right now
correct?

00:00:05.939 --> 00:00:08.675 size:84% position:42%
-Yep, I'm in my home
in Burlington, Vermont.

00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:14.080
-It must often be nice to be
back out of D.C., in Vermont

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:15.648 line:92%
But obviously the circumstance

00:00:15.648 --> 00:00:17.083 align:start size:94% position:6%
are a little different
right now.

00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:18.952 line:92%
How have you been balancin

00:00:18.952 --> 00:00:22.722 align:start size:94% position:6%
both doing your Senate wor
and your campaign?

00:00:22.722 --> 00:00:23.623 size:81% position:41% line:92%
-With much difficulty.

00:00:23.623 --> 00:00:25.859 align:start size:94% position:6%
It is a very, very strange tim
for me.

00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:29.062
I think it's a strange tim
for the American people.

00:00:29.062 --> 00:00:31.931 size:78% position:39%
The campaign has been
radically changed.

00:00:31.931 --> 00:00:32.665 size:75% position:38% line:92%
We can't do rallies.

00:00:32.665 --> 00:00:35.568 align:start size:94% position:6%
We can't go out
and do door-to-door stuff,

00:00:35.568 --> 00:00:36.770 line:92%
which is what we love to do.

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:40.106
As a senator, I can't meet
in person with my staff.

00:00:40.106 --> 00:00:44.110 align:start size:94% position:6%
So it is -- you know,
it's a strange moment for me

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:46.146 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think for you,
normally we would not

00:00:46.146 --> 00:00:48.281 align:start size:94% position:6%
be doing the show
from your home.

00:00:48.281 --> 00:00:53.887
It's a strange, unprecedente
moment for the American people

00:00:53.887 --> 00:00:57.123 align:start size:94% position:6%
-One of the moments
or one of the unique thing

00:00:57.123 --> 00:00:59.692 align:start size:94% position:6%
about this moment is how it'
making a lot of people

00:00:59.692 --> 00:01:02.062 align:start size:94% position:6%
re-evaluate the current system
in healthcare.

00:01:02.062 --> 00:01:04.597 align:start size:94% position:6%
Obviously Medicare for all
is the issue

00:01:04.597 --> 00:01:05.765 line:92%
people most associate you with

00:01:05.765 --> 00:01:09.069 align:start size:94% position:6%
Could you just speak to ho
this moment would be different

00:01:09.069 --> 00:01:12.672
if we had a system like Medicare
for all and the shortcomings

00:01:12.672 --> 00:01:14.107 size:78% position:39%
of the current system
we're living with?

00:01:14.107 --> 00:01:16.376 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, thank you for askin
that question, Seth.

00:01:16.376 --> 00:01:18.545 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think the answer
is fairly obvious.

00:01:18.545 --> 00:01:23.149
Right now, as we speak, millions
of people are losing their jobs.

00:01:23.149 --> 00:01:26.719 align:start size:94% position:6%
And some 87 million people
already did not have

00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:29.289 size:75% position:38%
any health insurance
or are underinsured.

00:01:29.289 --> 00:01:32.292
So people are sitting home
right now, scared to death

00:01:32.292 --> 00:01:33.193 line:92%
that somebody in their famil

00:01:33.193 --> 00:01:34.594 align:start size:94% position:6%
is going to come down
with the virus.

00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:37.464
They don't know how they wil
even pay for the treatment

00:01:37.464 --> 00:01:41.701
they receive let alone any other
problems their families have

00:01:41.701 --> 00:01:46.806
Second of all, people are asking
how does it happen when we spend

00:01:46.806 --> 00:01:49.709 align:start size:94% position:6%
twice as much per person o
healthcare as any other nation

00:01:49.709 --> 00:01:55.949
And yet our public health system
is so weak, so incredibly weak

00:01:55.949 --> 00:01:58.718 align:start size:94% position:6%
that we have
doctors and nurses right now

00:01:58.718 --> 00:02:01.888
who don't have the masks,
don't have the gloves,

00:02:01.888 --> 00:02:04.524 align:start size:94% position:6%
don't have the gowns,
the protective equipment

00:02:04.524 --> 00:02:09.129 align:start size:94% position:6%
they need to keep themselves
safe and do the work

00:02:09.129 --> 00:02:09.896 size:78% position:39% line:92%
that they have to do.

00:02:09.896 --> 00:02:14.667
A Medicare-for-all system is
designed to provide quality care

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:19.439 align:start size:94% position:6%
for all to do preventive wor
in order to prepare

00:02:19.439 --> 00:02:23.877
for some types of pandemics,
not simply to make huge amount

00:02:23.877 --> 00:02:26.212 align:start size:94% position:6%
of money
for the insurance companie

00:02:26.212 --> 00:02:26.946 size:84% position:42% line:92%
and the drug companies.

00:02:26.946 --> 00:02:31.184 align:start size:94% position:6%
-This, again, moment in time
led to a bipartisan bill

00:02:31.184 --> 00:02:34.087 align:start size:94% position:6%
being passed, a bill
that the President signed.

00:02:34.087 --> 00:02:37.257 align:start size:94% position:6%
You still used your time
on the Senate floor

00:02:37.257 --> 00:02:40.827
to be a bit sarcastic toward
your Republican colleagues

00:02:40.827 --> 00:02:42.262 line:92%
We do enjoy your sarcasm.

00:02:42.262 --> 00:02:44.898 size:88% position:44%
We feel like it's when
your Brooklyn comes out.

00:02:44.898 --> 00:02:48.034 align:start size:94% position:6%
You were referring to the fact
that some across the aisle

00:02:48.034 --> 00:02:52.972
felt that the bill was being too
generous to the less fortunate

00:02:52.972 --> 00:02:54.107 size:75% position:38% line:92%
to poorer Americans.

00:02:54.107 --> 00:02:58.578
In those moments of sarcasm,
is that -- is that just maskin

00:02:58.578 --> 00:03:03.249
a simmering rage you feel when
your colleagues react like that?

00:03:03.249 --> 00:03:03.983 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Absolutely.

00:03:03.983 --> 00:03:06.319 align:start size:94% position:6%
Look, you have folks
in the Senate,

00:03:06.319 --> 00:03:10.590
my Republican colleagues,
who voted for a trillion dollars

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:13.993 size:88% position:44%
in tax breaks for the 1%
and large corporations.

00:03:13.993 --> 00:03:18.464
And yet in this stimulus package
where we fought to make sure

00:03:18.464 --> 00:03:23.636
that we expand unemployment,
by the way, to a lot more people

00:03:23.636 --> 00:03:25.505 align:start size:94% position:6%
than previously
were eligible for it,

00:03:25.505 --> 00:03:29.909 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then what we said is
that over a four-month perio

00:03:29.909 --> 00:03:31.744 size:81% position:41% line:92%
in this terrible time,

00:03:31.744 --> 00:03:34.214
when people are so worried about
how they're going to

00:03:34.214 --> 00:03:36.249 size:78% position:39%
feed their families,
et cetera, et cetera,

00:03:36.249 --> 00:03:39.886 size:84% position:42%
that for four months
we're going to add $600

00:03:39.886 --> 00:03:44.724 align:start size:94% position:6%
to what you would normally get
from an unemployment check

00:03:44.724 --> 00:03:48.027 align:start size:94% position:6%
So if you get 350, 400 bucks
which is about average,

00:03:48.027 --> 00:03:51.030
you know what, you're gettin
another $600 on top of that.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:54.167
And we had some of my Republican
colleagues that said, "Imagine

00:03:54.167 --> 00:03:58.671
there would be some low-income
workers who would actually ear

00:03:58.671 --> 00:04:02.475
more from their unemployment
check than they previously did

00:04:02.475 --> 00:04:05.378
when they were making, you know,
10, 12 bucks an hour.

00:04:05.378 --> 00:04:06.746 line:92%
We can't allow that to happen.

00:04:06.746 --> 00:04:11.718
And to me that is so ugly,
so grotesque, so immoral,

00:04:11.718 --> 00:04:14.621 size:81% position:41%
that I felt compelled
to speak out about it.

00:04:14.621 --> 00:04:17.490 align:start size:94% position:6%
-One of the other things that'
always been a criticism

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:21.728 align:start size:94% position:6%
about the sort of healthcare
that you've proposed

00:04:21.728 --> 00:04:24.597
is people will say, "Oh, there's
going to be rationed care.

00:04:24.597 --> 00:04:25.665 line:92%
There's going to be long lines

00:04:25.665 --> 00:04:27.033 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's going to be
death panels."

00:04:27.033 --> 00:04:30.637 align:start size:94% position:6%
And yet it does seem like no
there is --

00:04:30.637 --> 00:04:32.772 align:start size:94% position:6%
Certain people on the righ
make the argument that,

00:04:32.772 --> 00:04:35.942 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Hey, this is just how it goes
during a pandemic.

00:04:35.942 --> 00:04:38.344 align:start size:94% position:6%
Some people
who are most at risk,

00:04:38.344 --> 00:04:43.383 align:start size:94% position:6%
you have to sort of make a
economic decision about how --

00:04:43.383 --> 00:04:45.151 align:start size:94% position:6%
what value to put on
their lives."

00:04:45.151 --> 00:04:46.786 align:start size:94% position:6%
Is that stunning to you
when you hear that?

00:04:46.786 --> 00:04:48.721 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Of course it is stunning.
It is stunning.

00:04:48.721 --> 00:04:52.292 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it speaks to
the hypocrisy of these folks

00:04:52.292 --> 00:04:55.395 align:start size:94% position:6%
The truth is,
we should ask ourselves

00:04:55.395 --> 00:04:57.897 align:start size:94% position:6%
how in the wealthiest countr
on Earth,

00:04:57.897 --> 00:05:00.600 line:92%
not only our doctors and nurse

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.302 align:start size:94% position:6%
don't have the equipment
they need,

00:05:02.302 --> 00:05:05.505
our EMT people, these are people
on ambulances now,

00:05:05.505 --> 00:05:09.008
putting their lives on the line,
doing incredibly important work,

00:05:09.008 --> 00:05:12.378
the cops, the firemen,
and they're getting sick.

00:05:12.378 --> 00:05:13.780 align:start size:94% position:6%
And in some cases
they're getting sick

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:15.615 align:start size:94% position:6%
because they don't have
the equipment that they need

00:05:15.615 --> 00:05:19.686
We don't have enough ventilators
in certain hospitals, ICU units.

00:05:19.686 --> 00:05:22.755 align:start size:94% position:6%
How does that happen in
the richest country on Earth

00:05:22.755 --> 00:05:27.193 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I think what this brings us
to the conclusion is

00:05:27.193 --> 00:05:29.662 align:start size:94% position:6%
that the function of healthcar
should not be to make

00:05:29.662 --> 00:05:33.599
$100 billion in profits last
year for the drug companie

00:05:33.599 --> 00:05:34.834 line:92%
and the insurance companies.

00:05:34.834 --> 00:05:37.370 line:92%
It should be to create a syste

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:39.372 align:start size:94% position:6%
which guarantees
healthcare to all,

00:05:39.372 --> 00:05:43.910 align:start size:94% position:6%
a system which has a stron
public health component

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:47.246
so that we have doctors an
nurses all over this country

00:05:47.246 --> 00:05:51.217
where today we are in --
have a lot of underserved areas.

00:05:51.217 --> 00:05:55.421
And that we make sure that
healthcare is a human right.

00:05:55.421 --> 00:05:56.756 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's, I think, where
we've got to go.

00:05:56.756 --> 00:05:58.925 align:start size:94% position:6%
The other thing,
if I may say so, Seth,

00:05:58.925 --> 00:06:02.995 align:start size:94% position:6%
is that in this terrible,
unprecedented moment,

00:06:02.995 --> 00:06:05.598 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think it's appropriate
for us to ask

00:06:05.598 --> 00:06:07.934 align:start size:94% position:6%
how we got here
and where we want to go.

00:06:07.934 --> 00:06:10.036 align:start size:94% position:6%
What are the lessons
that we're learning from this?

00:06:10.036 --> 00:06:12.672 size:81% position:41%
When this is all over,
and it will be over,

00:06:12.672 --> 00:06:16.309 align:start size:94% position:6%
do we simply go back
to the same old, same old?

00:06:16.309 --> 00:06:17.143 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
And I hope not.

00:06:17.143 --> 00:06:22.515
I hope that we ask why it is
that at a time of massive income

00:06:22.515 --> 00:06:23.316 size:81% position:41% line:92%
and wealth inequality,

00:06:23.316 --> 00:06:27.186 align:start size:94% position:6%
half of our people are livin
paycheck to paycheck.

00:06:27.186 --> 00:06:29.389
And what we're not talking about
right now, Seth,

00:06:29.389 --> 00:06:34.460
is that if you are making 14
bucks an hour, 12 bucks an hour,

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:37.930 align:start size:94% position:6%
and that check stops coming in
which is the case,

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:39.432 align:start size:94% position:6%
how are you going to
feed your family?

00:06:39.432 --> 00:06:41.033 align:start size:94% position:6%
How are you going to
pay the rent?

00:06:41.033 --> 00:06:43.569 align:start size:94% position:6%
How are you going to keep
your telephone going?

00:06:43.569 --> 00:06:44.771 line:92%
We're not talking about that

00:06:44.771 --> 00:06:47.874 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we need another
stimulus package, by the way

00:06:47.874 --> 00:06:49.609 line:92%
which addresses those issues

00:06:49.609 --> 00:06:53.146 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we need to rethink
after this epidemic is ove

00:06:53.146 --> 00:06:55.314 size:88% position:44% line:92%
where America should be.

